    "product_id": "a0eac925-c12a-483b-9f1c-58806d998af2",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 111752\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1052 AM PDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n*  Well above normal temperatures continue Friday, then temperatures \n   drop 5-10 degrees this weekend. \n\n*  Isolated convection possible in Northern Mojave and Lincoln \n   Counties Friday. \n\n*  Breezy south-southwesterly wind gusts expected across the\n   region Friday through the weekend. With reduced humidities \n   leading to fire weather concerns.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Early Next Week\n\nWhile Thursday will likely have been the hottest day of the week, \nwell above normal temperatures continue Friday before temperatures \ncool down this weekend by 5-10 degrees. A hot airmass is currently \nin place across the region with 850mb temperature anomalies well \nabove normal for this time of year, supporting highs at or above 100 \nin desert valleys and in the CRV. The strong ridge which built in \noverhead earlier in the week is showing signs of breaking down as a \ntrough pushes into the PNW. By Friday, the 500mb high shifts \nsoutheast into AZ, then continues eastward through the weekend \nbecoming centered over Southern New Mexico while the PNW trough \npushes inland. Influence from the incoming trough, lowering upper \nheights, and strong subsidence shifting out of the region should all \nresult in cooling surface temperatures across the Mojave Desert and \nGreat Basin. Low and mid level temperature anomalies still remain \nabove normal for this time of year, so still expect daily highs and \nlows above normal as well...but not at the levels seen this past \nweek. Lingering monsoonal moisture will also help keep nighttime \nlows elevated the next few days with lows in the 80s likely in \ndesert valleys, preventing much relief from the heat. By next week, \na persistent troughing pattern looks to set up over the west coast, \nkeeping southwesterly flow aloft locally and preventing strong \nridging from developing overhead. Medium to long range guidance \nkeeps low and mid level temperature anomalies and upper level height \nanomalies near to slightly above normal for this time of year into \nnext week. This raises confidence that temperatures will continue to \nrun above normal by a few degrees for both highs and lows into the \nextended period; however with Heat Risk remaining low to moderate, \nnot expecting dangerous heat conditions to return in this pattern. \nHighs generally in the 90s with lows in the 70s can be expected in \ndesert valleys, the CRV, and death valley in the extended while \ncooler temperatures are likely at higher elevations. \n\nAs mentioned above, lingering monsoonal moisture still remains in \nplace for portions of the region just ahead of the trough. Hi-res \nguidance shows the potential for scattered convection in portions of \nLincoln and Mohave counties Friday as a weak disturbance passes \nthrough. Added chance wording in the forecast to account for this \nthreat, but not expecting much impacts from anything that does \ndevelop. Brief periods of heavy rain, lightning, and gusty winds \npossible with any storm that does develop. \n\nThe aforementioned trough pushing into the western US will also \nincrease southwest winds across the region. Guidance continues to \nshow a 40 kt mid level jet streak developing overhead Saturday into \nSunday. Mixing into this area of stronger winds will result in \nwidespread gusts mixing down to the surface 30-35 mph. There could \nalso be some gusty southerly winds impacting Lake Mohave and Lake \nHavasu this weekend, which could impact boaters with winds and \nwaves. Since the low to mid level jet ramps up during the overnight \nhours Friday and Saturday night, the strongest winds in the region \nmay occur in wind prone areas of San Bernardino county with the \nnightly westerly push and also at higher elevations across the \nregion. Looking at today's Ensembles and NBM probabilities, all \nguidance has kept the threat relatively low for gusts over 40mph. \nWhile it does still look breezy with frequent gusts over 30mph, \nlatest trends lower confidence in frequent gusts over 40 mph and \nthe possibility of needing a wind advisory. Still will monitor \nforecast trends through the week and will update the forecast \naccordingly. \n\nThis coupled with very dry conditions has increase fire weather \nconcerns, with a Fire Weather Watch in effect for Nye, Esmeralda, \nand portions of Lincoln and Clark counties for Saturday. Winds look \nto reduce for Sunday but remain breezy. Longer term models are \nstarting to favor breezy winds peaking around 25 mph each afternoon \ninto the first half of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast \nPackage...Breezy southerly to southwesterly winds will continue \nthrough the TAF period along with wind gusts in the 15 to 25 knot \nrange. VFR conditions will persist through the TAF period. \nTemperatures will exceed through 02Z. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Gusty southerly to \nsouthwesterly winds will pick up across the region early this \nafternoon with peak wind gusts in the 20 to 25 knot range for most\nlocations. Stronger, westerly winds are expected across the \nwestern Mojave Desert, including KDAG, with peak gusts in the 25 \nto 30 knot range. Northwesterly winds in the northern Owens \nValley, will briefly increase out of the west later this afternoon\nbefore shifting back to the northwest after sunset. FEW to SCT \nmid and high clouds will filter through the area. Isolated \nthunderstorms may develop this afternoon over parts of Mohave and \nLincoln counties, but these should not impact any terminals. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Moore\nAVIATION...Stessman\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
